LOGGER(1)                        User Commands                       LOGGER(1)



NNAAMMEE
       logger - enter messages into the system log

SSYYNNOOPPSSIISS
       llooggggeerr [options] _m_e_s_s_a_g_e

DDEESSCCRRIIPPTTIIOONN
       llooggggeerr makes entries in the system log.

       When the optional _m_e_s_s_a_g_e argument is present, it is written to the
       log. If it is not present, and the --ff option is not given either, then
       standard input is logged.

OOPPTTIIOONNSS
       --dd, ----uuddpp
           Use datagrams (UDP) only. By default the connection is tried to the
           syslog port defined in _/_e_t_c_/_s_e_r_v_i_c_e_s, which is often 514.

           See also ----sseerrvveerr and ----ssoocckkeett to specify where to connect.

       --ee, ----sskkiipp--eemmppttyy
           Ignore empty lines when processing files. An empty line is defined
           to be a line without any characters. Thus a line consisting only of
           whitespace is NOT considered empty. Note that when the
           ----pprriioo--pprreeffiixx option is specified, the priority is not part of the
           line. Thus an empty line in this mode is a line that does not have
           any characters after the priority prefix (e.g., <<1133>>).

       --ff, ----ffiillee _f_i_l_e
           Log the contents of the specified _f_i_l_e. This option cannot be
           combined with a command-line message.

       --ii
           Log the PID of the llooggggeerr process with each line.

       ----iidd[==_i_d]
           Log the PID of the llooggggeerr process with each line. When the optional
           argument _i_d is specified, then it is used instead of the llooggggeerr
           command’s PID. The use of ----iidd==$$$$ (PPID) is recommended in scripts
           that send several messages.

           Note that the system logging infrastructure (for example ssyysstteemmdd
           when listening on _/_d_e_v_/_l_o_g) may follow local socket credentials to
           overwrite the PID specified in the message. llooggggeerr(1) is able to
           set those socket credentials to the given _i_d, but only if you have
           root permissions and a process with the specified PID exists,
           otherwise the socket credentials are not modified and the problem
           is silently ignored.

       ----jjoouurrnnaalldd[==_f_i_l_e]
           Write a ssyysstteemmdd journal entry. The entry is read from the given
           _f_i_l_e, when specified, otherwise from standard input. Each line must
           begin with a field that is accepted by jjoouurrnnaalldd; see
           ssyysstteemmdd..jjoouurrnnaall--ffiieellddss(7) for details. The use of a MESSAGE_ID
           field is generally a good idea, as it makes finding entries easy.
           Examples:

              logger --journald <<end
              MESSAGE_ID=67feb6ffbaf24c5cbec13c008dd72309
              MESSAGE=The dogs bark, but the caravan goes on.
              DOGS=bark
              CARAVAN=goes on
              end

              logger --journald=entry.txt

       Notice that ----jjoouurrnnaalldd will ignore values of other options, such as
       priority. If priority is needed it must be within input, and use
       PRIORITY field. The simple execution of jjoouurrnnaallccttll(1) will display
       MESSAGE field. Use jjoouurrnnaallccttll ----oouuttppuutt jjssoonn--pprreettttyy to see rest of the
       fields.

       + To include newlines in MESSAGE, specify MESSAGE several times. This
       is handled as a special case, other fields will be stored as an array
       in the journal if they appear multiple times.

       ----mmssggiidd _m_s_g_i_d
           Sets the RFC 5424 <https://tools.ietf.org/html/rfc5424> MSGID
           field. Note that the space character is not permitted inside of
           _m_s_g_i_d. This option is only used if ----rrffcc55442244 is specified as well;
           otherwise, it is silently ignored.

       --nn, ----sseerrvveerr _s_e_r_v_e_r
           Write to the specified remote syslog _s_e_r_v_e_r instead of to the
           system log socket. Unless ----uuddpp or ----ttccpp is specified, llooggggeerr will
           first try to use UDP, but if this fails a TCP connection is
           attempted.

       ----nnoo--aacctt
           Causes everything to be done except for writing the log message to
           the system log, and removing the connection to the journal. This
           option can be used together with ----ssttddeerrrr for testing purposes.

       ----oocctteett--ccoouunntt
           Use the RFC 6587 <https://tools.ietf.org/html/rfc6587> octet
           counting framing method for sending messages. When this option is
           not used, the default is no framing on UDP, and RFC6587
           non-transparent framing (also known as octet stuffing) on TCP.

       --PP, ----ppoorrtt _p_o_r_t
           Use the specified _p_o_r_t. When this option is not specified, the port
           defaults to ssyysslloogg for udp and to ssyysslloogg--ccoonnnn for tcp connections.

       --pp, ----pprriioorriittyy _p_r_i_o_r_i_t_y
           Enter the message into the log with the specified _p_r_i_o_r_i_t_y. The
           priority may be specified numerically or as a _f_a_c_i_l_i_t_y._l_e_v_e_l pair.
           For example, --pp llooccaall33..iinnffoo logs the message as informational in
           the local3 facility. The default is uusseerr..nnoottiiccee.

       ----pprriioo--pprreeffiixx
           Look for a syslog prefix on every line read from standard input.
           This prefix is a decimal number within angle brackets that encodes
           both the facility and the level. The number is constructed by
           multiplying the facility by 8 and then adding the level. For
           example, llooccaall00..iinnffoo, meaning facility=16 and level=6, becomes
           <<113344>>.

           If the prefix contains no facility, the facility defaults to what
           is specified by the --pp option. Similarly, if no prefix is provided,
           the line is logged using the _p_r_i_o_r_i_t_y given with --pp.

           This option doesn’t affect a command-line message.

       ----rrffcc33116644
           Use the RFC 3164 <https://tools.ietf.org/html/rfc3164> BSD syslog
           protocol to submit messages to a remote server.

       ----rrffcc55442244[==_w_i_t_h_o_u_t]
           Use the RFC 5424 <https://tools.ietf.org/html/rfc5424> syslog
           protocol to submit messages to a remote server. The optional
           _w_i_t_h_o_u_t argument can be a comma-separated list of the following
           values: nnoottqq, nnoottiimmee, nnoohhoosstt.

           The nnoottqq value suppresses the time-quality structured data from the
           submitted message. The time-quality information shows whether the
           local clock was synchronized plus the maximum number of
           microseconds the timestamp might be off. The time quality is also
           automatically suppressed when ----ssdd--iidd ttiimmeeQQuuaalliittyy is specified.

           The nnoottiimmee value (which implies nnoottqq) suppresses the complete
           sender timestamp that is in ISO-8601 format, including microseconds
           and timezone.

           The nnoohhoosstt value suppresses ggeetthhoossttnnaammee(2) information from the
           message header.

           The RFC 5424 protocol has been the default for llooggggeerr since version
           2.26.

       --ss, ----ssttddeerrrr
           Output the message to standard error as well as to the system log.

       ----ssdd--iidd _n_a_m_e[@@_d_i_g_i_t_s]
           Specifies a structured data element ID for an RFC 5424 message
           header. The option has to be used before ----ssdd--ppaarraamm to introduce a
           new element. The number of structured data elements is unlimited.
           The ID (_n_a_m_e plus possibly @@_d_i_g_i_t_s) is case-sensitive and uniquely
           identifies the type and purpose of the element. The same ID must
           not exist more than once in a message. The @@_d_i_g_i_t_s part is required
           for user-defined non-standardized IDs.

           llooggggeerr currently generates the ttiimmeeQQuuaalliittyy standardized element
           only. RFC 5424 also describes the elements oorriiggiinn (with parameters
           iipp, eenntteerrpprriisseeIIdd, ssooffttwwaarree and sswwVVeerrssiioonn) and mmeettaa (with parameters
           sseeqquueenncceeIIdd, ssyyssUUppTTiimmee and llaanngguuaaggee). These element IDs may be
           specified without the @@_d_i_g_i_t_s suffix.

       ----ssdd--ppaarraamm _n_a_m_e=_v_a_l_u_e
           Specifies a structured data element parameter, a name and value
           pair. The option has to be used after ----ssdd--iidd and may be specified
           more than once for the same element. Note that the quotation marks
           around _v_a_l_u_e are required and must be escaped on the command line.

                   logger --rfc5424 --sd-id zoo@123             \
                                    --sd-param tiger="hungry"   \
                                    --sd-param zebra="running"  \
                                    --sd-id manager@123         \
                                    --sd-param onMeeting="yes"  \
                                    "this is message"

           produces:

           <<1133>>11 22001155--1100--0011TT1144::0077::5599..116688666622++0022::0000 wwss kkzzaakk -- -- [[ttiimmeeQQuuaalliittyy
           ttzzKKnnoowwnn==""11"" iissSSyynncceedd==""11"" ssyynnccAAccccuurraaccyy==""221188661166""]][[zzoooo@@112233
           ttiiggeerr==""hhuunnggrryy"" zzeebbrraa==""rruunnnniinngg""]][[mmaannaaggeerr@@112233 oonnMMeeeettiinngg==""yyeess""]] tthhiiss
           iiss mmeessssaaggee

       --SS, ----ssiizzee _s_i_z_e
           Sets the maximum permitted message size to _s_i_z_e. The default is
           1KiB characters, which is the limit traditionally used and
           specified in RFC 3164. With RFC 5424, this limit has become
           flexible. A good assumption is that RFC 5424 receivers can at least
           process 4KiB messages.

           Most receivers accept messages larger than 1KiB over any type of
           syslog protocol. As such, the ----ssiizzee option affects llooggggeerr in all
           cases (not only when ----rrffcc55442244 was used).

           Note: the message-size limit limits the overall message size,
           including the syslog header. Header sizes vary depending on the
           selected options and the hostname length. As a rule of thumb,
           headers are usually not longer than 50 to 80 characters. When
           selecting a maximum message size, it is important to ensure that
           the receiver supports the max size as well, otherwise messages may
           become truncated. Again, as a rule of thumb two to four KiB message
           size should generally be OK, whereas anything larger should be
           verified to work.

       ----ssoocckkeett--eerrrroorrss[==_m_o_d_e]
           Print errors about Unix socket connections. The _m_o_d_e can be a value
           of ooffff, oonn, or aauuttoo. When the mode is aauuttoo, then llooggggeerr will detect
           if the init process is ssyysstteemmdd(1), and if so assumption is made
           _/_d_e_v_/_l_o_g can be used early at boot. Other init systems lack of
           _/_d_e_v_/_l_o_g will not cause errors that is identical with messaging
           using ooppeennlloogg(3) system call. The llooggggeerr(1) before version 2.26
           used ooppeennlloogg(3), and hence was unable to detected loss of messages
           sent to Unix sockets.

           The default mode is aauuttoo. When errors are not enabled lost messages
           are not communicated and will result to successful exit status of
           llooggggeerr(1) invocation.

       --TT, ----ttccpp
           Use stream (TCP) only. By default the connection is tried to the
           _s_y_s_l_o_g_-_c_o_n_n port defined in _/_e_t_c_/_s_e_r_v_i_c_e_s, which is often _6_0_1.

           See also ----sseerrvveerr and ----ssoocckkeett to specify where to connect.

       --tt, ----ttaagg _t_a_g
           Mark every line to be logged with the specified _t_a_g. The default
           tag is the name of the user logged in on the terminal (or a user
           name based on effective user ID).

       --uu, ----ssoocckkeett _s_o_c_k_e_t
           Write to the specified _s_o_c_k_e_t instead of to the system log socket.

       ----
           End the argument list. This allows the _m_e_s_s_a_g_e to start with a
           hyphen (-).

       --hh, ----hheellpp
           Display help text and exit.

       --VV, ----vveerrssiioonn
           Print version and exit.

